;; A body-local `defbjo`.
;;
;; Still refused, and the reason has moved. It used to be that a local function
;; could not suspend at all; now it can — `158_local_functions.bjo` is the whole
;; group of that — so what is refused here is the *declaration*, not the thing
;; declared.
;;
;; Which is the language's own rule rather than a limit of the emitter. Colour
;; is declared where a signature is required, and a body-local definition has
;; no signature: it takes its colour from what its body reaches. `defbjo` here
;; would be a second spelling with one behaviour, and would leave the reader
;; thinking the first spelling does not suspend.
;;
;; So the advice is the point of the message: write `defun`, and it suspends.

;; EXPECT-ERROR: bjoroutine may only be defined at the top level
;; EXPECT-ERROR: takes its colour from what its body reaches

(: outer (-> int int))
(defbjo (outer x)
  (defbjo (inner n) (* n 2))
  (inner x))

(defun (main args) 0)
